<DocsLayout title=".holo files" description="The physical layout, logical layers, identity model, packaging profiles, and verification rules of Hologram v4 application archives.">
  <h1><code>.holo</code> files</h1>
  <p>A <code>.holo</code> file is Hologram's native executable and application archive. Version 4 represents a Wasm program, tensor plan, root filesystem, view, callable inference model, or a composed application containing several of those layers. Live accepts only version 4.</p>

  <div class="note"><strong>Sections are not layers</strong>The physical file has a header and section table. Logical application layers live inside the canonical <code>AppManifest</code> section and point to content by κ. A layer does not receive a second physical header of its own.</div>

  <h2>Two views of one archive</h2>
  <div class="doc-grid">
    <article class="doc-card"><h3>Physical view</h3><p>Header, section-table entries, payload sections, and the verified footer. This view lets the loader find byte ranges safely.</p></article>
    <article class="doc-card"><h3>Application view</h3><p>Requirements, ordered layers, child applications, and κ references. This view defines what the application means.</p></article>
  </div>

  <pre><code>.holo v4
├─ header                         magic · version · flags · section count
├─ section table                 kind · offset · length, repeated
├─ AppManifest section           primary · requires · layers · children
├─ Extension section             verified application directory
├─ Metadata section              source compile manifest, when present
├─ ContentBlob section × N       κ71 · content bytes
├─ other execution sections      plans · weights · ports · certificates · …
└─ footer                         32-byte BLAKE3 fingerprint</code></pre>

  <h2>Physical binary layout</h2>
  <p>All integer fields are little-endian. The header is followed immediately by <code>section_count</code> fixed-width entries. Offsets are absolute byte offsets from the beginning of the file.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Offset</th><th>Size</th><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>4 bytes</td><td><code>magic</code></td><td>ASCII <code>HOLO</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>4</code></td><td>2 bytes</td><td><code>format_version</code></td><td>Current writers emit version <code>4</code>; readers accept versions 2–4.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>6</code></td><td>2 bytes</td><td><code>flags</code></td><td>Reserved format flags.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>8</code></td><td>2 bytes</td><td><code>section_count</code></td><td>Number of section-table entries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>10</code></td><td>24 bytes each</td><td><code>section[]</code></td><td><code>kind:u8</code>, seven padding bytes, <code>offset:u64</code>, and <code>length:u64</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>After table</td><td>Variable</td><td>Payloads</td><td>Section bytes in table order.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Final 32 bytes</td><td>32 bytes</td><td>Fingerprint</td><td>BLAKE3 over every preceding archive byte.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>Section kinds</h3>
  <p>The section kind is a closed numeric discriminant. New meanings require an additive format change; producer-specific metadata belongs in repeatable <code>Extension</code> sections.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Kind</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td><code>KernelCalls</code></td><td>Compiled tensor operations.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td><code>Schedule</code></td><td>Tensor execution schedule.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td><code>Weights</code></td><td>Encoded tensor weights.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4–5</td><td><code>ShapeRegistry</code>, <code>DTypeRegistry</code></td><td>Tensor shape and data-type registries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6–8</td><td><code>Certificates</code>, <code>Trace</code>, <code>Metadata</code></td><td>Verification, trace, and producer metadata.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9–11</td><td><code>Inputs</code>, <code>Outputs</code>, <code>Constants</code></td><td>Typed execution ports and constant values.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12–13</td><td><code>ExecPlan</code>, <code>WarmStart</code></td><td>Parallel execution levels and warm-start material.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>14</td><td><code>Extension</code></td><td>Keyed, producer-defined bytes. Repeatable.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>15</td><td><code>AppManifest</code></td><td>Canonical application structure.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>16</td><td><code>ContentBlob</code></td><td>A 71-byte κ label followed by its content bytes. Repeatable.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The application manifest</h2>
  <p><code>AppManifest</code> is an IRI-tagged canonical realization. Its embedded κ operands make application closure discoverable without giving human names authority over identity.</p>
  <pre><code>AppManifest
├─ primary: 0                    layer whose exit code is the app exit code
├─ requires: κ                   requested CapabilitySet
├─ layer[0]: wasm · κ · holo_run
├─ layer[1]: tensor · κ · session
├─ layer[2]: rootfs · κ · boot · arm64
├─ layer[3]: view · κ · portable
├─ layer[4]: inference-model · κ · ai.default · uor-r4
└─ child[0]: application κ · delegated capabilities κ</code></pre>

  <h3>Layer kinds</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Source kind</th><th>Manifest kind</th><th>Descriptor</th><th>Hologram Live today</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>wasm</code></td><td><code>WasmCodemodule</code></td><td>Content κ and callable export; portable.</td><td>Executes in-process through the ordered provider lifecycle; the primary may be at any layer position.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tensor</code></td><td><code>TensorPlan</code></td><td>Content κ and session entrypoint; no app exit code.</td><td>Packages and inspects; execution backend remains explicit.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rootfs</code></td><td><code>RootfsImage</code></td><td>Content κ, entrypoint, and required architecture.</td><td>Directly executes experimental Python OCI bundles; other providers remain explicit.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>view</code></td><td><code>View</code></td><td>Canonical bundle κ and target surface; no app exit code.</td><td>Compiles and validates a portable asset directory; attaches when the host publishes a surface handle.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>inference-model</code></td><td><code>InferenceModel</code></td><td>Content κ, unique callable entry, and engine identifier; no app exit code.</td><td>Imports, verifies, packages, and inspects; provider invocation remains explicit.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Manifest order is initialization order. Only Wasm and rootfs layers have exit semantics and may be selected as <code>primary</code>; a model-only archive uses <code>primary = null</code>. Composition belongs in child applications rather than a second dependency graph among layers.</p>

  <h3>Portable View bundle v1</h3>
  <p>A View source path names a directory whose fixed entry is <code>index.html</code>. The compiler writes the <code>HOLOVIEW</code> magic, version 1, entry path, file count, then every regular asset as its portable UTF-8 path, byte length, and exact bytes in strict lexical order. It rejects symlinks, special files, missing entries, unsafe or case-colliding paths, unsupported versions, truncation, trailing bytes, and bounded-size violations. Host paths, timestamps, permissions, ownership, empty directories, MIME guesses, and creation order are absent, so equivalent trees produce identical content κ values. Only surface <code>portable</code> is accepted. The host-neutral provider validates in <code>prepare</code>, attaches the currently published portable handle in <code>start</code>, and detaches idempotently in reverse shutdown. Desktop publishes that handle and serves assets from a window-bound opaque <code>hologram-view</code> origin with no filesystem or localhost exposure; application windows receive no Tauri capabilities and cannot navigate or open external windows. Their only application bridge is a same-origin, JSON-only <code>POST /_hologram/intent</code>: v1 admits the single bounded <code>application.invoke</code> text message and binds it to that application κ's prepared primary. CLI and server runtimes publish no UI surface, so <code>holo plan</code> reports an explicit direct/headless unavailable-surface blocker rather than a missing provider. ADR 018 defines the attachment and authority boundary.</p>

  <h2>Core-Wasm guest contract v1</h2>
  <p>A Wasm layer's canonical <code>entry</code> is authoritative. Under <code>core-wasm-v1</code>, the resolved module exports <code>memory</code>, <code>holo_alloc(len: i32) -&gt; i32</code>, and the declared entry with signature <code>(ptr: i32, len: i32) -&gt; i64</code>. The packed result is <code>out_ptr &lt;&lt; 32 | out_len</code>. <code>holo_run</code> is the source compiler and generator default, but direct and resident providers resolve whatever valid export the manifest declares and reject a missing or wrongly typed entry during preparation.</p>
  <p>V1 imports no WASI or Hologram host functions, creates a fresh instance for every input, and returns exactly one byte output per input. It has no numeric process exit code: returning bytes is successful completion, while a trap is a typed protocol error.</p>

  <h2>Guest-contract negotiation</h2>
  <p>The callable <code>entry</code> is not a version marker. Source-manifest schema v4 exposes a separate Wasm <code>contract</code> field which the compiler writes to canonical, identity-bearing <code>aux</code>. Empty remains the byte-compatible alias for <code>hologram:guest/core-wasm@1</code>; the accepted explicit identifiers are <code>hologram:guest/core-wasm@1</code>, <code>hologram:guest/component@1</code>, <code>hologram:guest/component-store-read@1</code>, <code>hologram:guest/component-store-graph-read@1</code>, <code>hologram:guest/component-store-write@1</code>, <code>hologram:guest/component-channel-publish@1</code>, <code>hologram:guest/component-channel-subscribe@1</code>, and <code>hologram:guest/component-network-fetch@1</code>. Inspection and planning always report the normalized identifier. Exact-major negotiation does not fall back, and older runtimes reject non-empty Wasm tags.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "schema_version": 4,
  "primary": 0,
  "layers": [&#123;
    "kind": "wasm",
    "path": "app.component.wasm",
    "entry": "run",
    "contract": "hologram:guest/component@1"
  &#125;]
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>The checked-in <code>hologram:application@1.0.0</code> WIT world exports one stateless binary <code>run</code> and imports nothing. Component archives compile, inspect, plan, and execute directly or resident through an exact-contract provider; they never route to core Wasm. Compiled code stays warm, but each input uses a fresh store. The host caps memory at 64 MiB, fuel at 100 million units per input, input and output at 1 MiB each, and each invocation at two seconds. Nonzero admitted memory and CPU-time scalars can only tighten those ceilings. Timeout and cancellation interrupt only the prepared component's engine. Unknown source identifiers are rejected before archive emission; an unknown canonical tag makes the archive invalid.</p>
  <p>The separate <code>component-store-read@1</code> profile imports exactly <code>hologram:host/store@1.0.0</code>. An application must request at least one exact object κ in <code>storage_roots</code>; Live proves those roots are contained by the trusted effective grant before constructing the linker, then checks every target before touching the object store. Direct execution uses the configured local registry, resident execution uses the catalog store, and a child retains only roots admitted by its delegated grant. The base Component world remains import-free, and the profile adds no filesystem, network, clock, environment, or other WASI interface. Remaining storage, channel, and mediated network imports require separate fixed contracts and corresponding canonical authority.</p>
  <p>The distinct <code>component-store-graph-read@1</code> profile keeps the same single read-only host import but resolves each admitted root through canonical typed UOR edges before linking. Every reachable object must exist locally and match its κ. Unknown types are opaque leaves; malformed or incomplete graphs and bounded depth, object, edge, or aggregate-byte limit failures are rejected before guest code starts. Existing exact-root read and write contracts do not widen, and child delegation remains an exact subset over graph roots.</p>
  <p>The separate <code>component-store-write@1</code> profile imports exactly <code>hologram:host/store-write@1.0.0</code> and cannot read. It requires at least one exact admitted destination κ plus nonzero <code>storage_quota_bytes</code> before linker construction. Every call verifies exact-root membership and that the supplied bytes hash to the expected κ. An atomic store operation charges only newly materialized bytes against a quota shared for the prepared application's lifetime; existing identical content costs nothing, and rejected calls leave no partial blob. Direct, resident, and delegated-child paths share these rules without adding a filesystem or WASI surface.</p>
  <p>The distinct <code>component-network-fetch@1</code> profile imports only <code>hologram:host/network-fetch@1.0.0</code> after a nonempty endpoint set is admitted. It exposes HTTPS GET with no guest methods, headers, credentials, cookies, proxy state, redirects, or raw sockets. Live checks canonical host/port/path containment per call, filters and pins DNS results to public destinations, and applies 2 KiB target, 1 MiB response, 1.5-second, and eight-operation concurrency ceilings. Direct and resident runtimes share the same policy; announce/listen remains unavailable.</p>
  <p>The channel profiles are separate publish-only and subscribe-only worlds. Each requires a nonempty exact request admitted through <code>publish_channels</code> or <code>subscribe_channels</code> and links only its matching host interface. A runtime-owned in-memory broker provides nonblocking 64 KiB messages and 64-entry FIFO mailboxes. Full publish returns backpressure without overwrite; one successful receive consumes one message and empty receive returns immediately. The v1 contract is at-most-once and has no replay, broadcast, acknowledgement, durable or cross-process delivery, or sockets.</p>

  <h2>Completion and exit</h2>
  <p>A run result carries output bytes and typed completion independently. <code>returned</code> means a callable such as core-Wasm v1 returned normally without a numeric process status. <code>exited</code> carries a code only when the provider observed a real process exit. Completion is required. Traps, nonzero process exits, protocol violations, and cleanup failures are operation errors, not successful completion values.</p>
  <p>Only the root primary supplies application completion. Child primaries and non-primary layers are lifecycle-managed dependencies. Wasm and rootfs may be exit-bearing; View, Tensor, and InferenceModel layers are non-exit-bearing and cannot become the runtime primary. Direct execution publishes completion only after reverse-order cleanup succeeds. Resident completion ends the current call but leaves the application loaded.</p>

  <h3>Capability requests</h3>
  <p>The <code>requires</code> operand points to an upstream canonical <code>CapabilitySet</code>, not to JSON. Authors may declare the request in a versioned <code>capabilities.json</code>; the compiler validates it and converts it to canonical realization bytes before calculating the κ stored in <code>AppManifest.requires</code>.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "schema_version": 2,
  "storage_roots": [],
  "storage_quota_bytes": 0,
  "network_fetch_endpoints": [],
  "network_announce_endpoints": [],
  "publish_channels": [],
  "subscribe_channels": [],
  "memory_max_bytes": 0,
  "cpu_time_per_event_ms": 0,
  "priority_weight": 0
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>κ arrays must be strictly sorted, contain no duplicates, and use canonical <code>blake3:&lt;64 lowercase hex&gt;</code> labels. Network arrays use exact canonical <code>https://lowercase-host:port/path-prefix</code> scopes, are also strictly sorted, and attenuate only by exact origin plus a path-segment boundary. Schema-1 documents remain accepted only when their legacy network booleans are absent or false; a true boolean is rejected rather than becoming ambient authority. Unknown fields and schema versions fail during both <code>compile --check</code> and <code>compile</code>, with the source path and invalid field/index in the diagnostic. Omitting <code>requires</code> produces the same canonical empty set as the document above. The upstream budget convention uses <code>0</code> for unbounded; set a nonzero budget to request a finite ceiling.</p>
  <p>Capability objects must always be canonical upstream <code>CapabilitySet</code> bytes. The canonical empty set is the only deny-all representation. Zero-byte and otherwise malformed objects are rejected even when their content address matches.</p>
  <div class="note"><strong>Request is not authority</strong>The capability object inside a <code>.holo</code> file is controlled by the application and only says what it requests. It is not an effective grant. Live decodes and admits the root request against a trusted effective grant, then proves every delegated child grant attenuates its parent's admitted grant and admits that child's request. Any failure occurs before provider preparation.</div>
  <p>The default local grant contains no storage roots, channels, or network endpoint scopes. A broader request fails with <code>LIVE_AUTHORIZATION_DENIED</code> before a provider prepares. Endpoint values never appear in audit rows or admission diagnostics. Defining a scope does not link networking: raw sockets remain unavailable and a bounded mediated-fetch profile is separate follow-up work. Direct local demos may opt in with <code>hologram run application.holo --development-grant development-grant.json</code>. Resident applications use only <code>holo.development_grant</code> from trusted service configuration; callers cannot attach self-asserted grants to a remote request.</p>
  <pre><code>hologram --json run application.holo \
  --development-grant development-grant.json |
  jq '&#123;authorization, grant_source, requested_capabilities_kappa, effective_grant_kappa&#125;'</code></pre>
  <p>Successful raw results report <code>authorization: "allowed"</code> plus the request κ, effective-grant κ, and one of <code>local_baseline</code>, <code>direct_development_file</code>, or <code>service_development_file</code>. Denials use the typed JSON error and structured trace path. Neither surface emits the source capability document.</p>

  <h3>Source languages do not add layer kinds</h3>
  <p>Python, Rust, Zig, and other source languages are compiler concerns. For example, a Python compiler can produce a <code>WasmCodemodule</code> containing a CPython WebAssembly component or a <code>RootfsImage</code> containing a locked Linux Python environment. The canonical application manifest records the resulting executable payload, not the workstation build recipe.</p>
  <p>This keeps the canonical layer model stable while source-manifest schemas and toolchains evolve independently. See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/python-apps')}>Python application guide</a> for the Component compiler's dependency-free and locked portable-wheel examples plus the native dependency rootfs compiler, and <a href={sitePath('/docs/model-apps')}>AI model applications</a> for the v4 service layer.</p>

  <h2>Queryable application directory</h2>
  <p>New Hologram Live archives carry the extension <code>https://hologram.foundation/extension/application-directory/v1</code>. Its deterministic JSON is a normalized projection of the manifest and physical packaging:</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "schema_version": 1,
  "primary_layer": 0,
  "requires_kappa": "blake3:…",
  "layers": [
    &#123;
      "position": 0,
      "kind": "wasm",
      "content_kappa": "blake3:…",
      "entry": "holo_run",
      "architecture": null,
      "surface": null,
      "engine": null
    &#125;
  ],
  "children": [],
  "blobs": [&#123; "kappa": "blake3:…", "byte_length": 842 &#125;]
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>The directory is a read model, not a second source of truth. Every v4 application archive must contain exactly one directory extension. During import and inspection, Live decodes the canonical manifest, hashes every embedded blob, derives this directory again, and rejects a missing, duplicate, malformed, or disagreeing directory. Structural archives without an application manifest may omit it. Model rows use the optional <code>engine</code> field.</p>

  <h2>Identity and verification</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Layer identity:</strong> each layer refers to content by its κ label.</li>
    <li><strong>Application κ (<code>application_kappa</code>):</strong> <code>address_bytes(AppManifest::canonicalize())</code>. It is unchanged when packaging changes between fat and thin, and absent when an archive has no application manifest.</li>
    <li><strong>Archive fingerprint (<code>archive_fingerprint</code>):</strong> the footer integrity value for the physical archive layout.</li>
    <li><strong>Archive object κ (<code>archive_kappa</code> in compile output, existing <code>kappa</code> in inspection):</strong> BLAKE3 over the complete file. It changes between fat and thin and is distinct from the footer fingerprint.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>hologram --json compile hologram.json --output application.holo |
  jq '&#123;archive_kappa, archive_fingerprint, application_kappa, capabilities_kappa&#125;'

hologram --json holo inspect application.holo |
  jq '&#123;kappa, archive_fingerprint, application_kappa&#125;'</code></pre>
  <p>Catalog lookup, import, verification, resident records, and run results identify a particular physical archive with <code>kappa</code>. Planning and manifest-graph diagnostics use <code>application_kappa</code>. Existing <code>kappa</code> fields keep their physical meaning.</p>
  <p>Source compilers may add <code>build_provenance</code> to their command result. Python provenance is schema-versioned and explicitly <code>canonical: false</code>: Component builds record source hashes, dependency artifacts, the exact host-specific componentizer wheel URL/SHA-256, observed tools, and the generated layer κ. Rootfs builds additionally retain the requested OCI base and report the digest-qualified registry reference actually used by Docker. This evidence is not a <code>.holo</code> section, directory field, content blob, or part of any identity above.</p>
  <p>Live rejects a bad footer, truncated or out-of-bounds payloads when accessed, an invalid manifest, duplicate blob labels, a blob whose κ does not match its bytes, duplicate application directories, unknown directory schema versions, and a directory that disagrees with the manifest.</p>

  <h2>Explain an execution plan</h2>
  <p><code>holo plan</code> resolves and verifies the complete root and child application closure without starting a provider. Give it a local path for a service-free direct plan, or an imported archive κ for a resident plan that may resolve thin payloads from the local content cache.</p>
  <pre><code>hologram --json holo plan ./application.holo |
  jq '&#123;application_kappa, packaging, runnable, layers, blockers&#125;'

hologram --json holo plan blake3:… |
  jq '&#123;execution_target, application_count, max_depth, resolved_object_count, resolved_bytes, runnable&#125;'</code></pre>
  <p>The report is deliberately payload-free. It contains the archive and application identities, <code>fat</code>/<code>thin</code>/<code>hybrid</code> packaging, root capability κ, ordered root-layer descriptors, <code>embedded</code> or <code>local_store</code> resolution sources, provider status, recursive application count and depth, and planning limits. Each child row identifies its parent and depth, application κ, delegated <code>capabilities_kappa</code>, requested <code>requires_kappa</code>, and both resolution sources. Unsupported providers return a successful explanation with <code>runnable: false</code>; each blocker has a stable <code>kind</code>, typed <code>error_code</code>, and human message. A fully resolved and provider-supported child tree is runnable. Invalid files and missing catalog IDs remain command errors.</p>

  <h2>Fat and thin packaging</h2>
  <div class="doc-grid">
    <article class="doc-card"><h3>Fat</h3><p>The manifest and referenced blobs travel together. It is self-contained and suited to distribution, air-gapped transfer, and first install.</p></article>
    <article class="doc-card"><h3>Thin</h3><p>The manifest travels without payload blobs. The runtime resolves the same κ references from a content store or sync source.</p></article>
  </div>
  <div class="note"><strong>Current Live behavior</strong><code>hologram compile</code> emits fat archives by default and <code>--thin</code> emits the same canonical manifest without content blobs. Importing a fat archive caches its verified payloads by κ, so catalog-backed resident execution can later resolve a thin archive. Before direct or resident execution starts a provider, Live iteratively resolves and re-hashes root and child manifests, requested and delegated capabilities, and every layer. Equal κ objects are deduplicated, while application depth/count and aggregate layer/object/byte limits cover the complete tree. It then admits the root and each parent → delegation → child-request chain and writes each allow/deny decision through the awaited JSONL audit boundary. Missing content, amplification, and under-granted requests therefore block root execution before provider preparation. Direct file execution requires a self-contained fat archive. An admitted tree starts as one transaction and shares one resident lifetime.</div>
  <p>Blocker-free applications use a closed provider registry keyed by the canonical layer kind. Providers are prepared and started depth-first in manifest order, with every child receiving only its admitted delegated grant. Unload and failure rollback stop the exact reverse order. The root primary is the only automatic invocation target; child primaries remain managed dependencies. The original typed failure remains primary if cleanup also fails. Wasmtime and the experimental Python OCI engine stay behind async native interfaces, so their private handles do not enter the plan or transport schema. Resident records expose lifecycle <code>state</code>, aggregate root-and-child resident bytes, queue depth, processed calls, and the request/grant κ, trusted source, and authorization outcome; repeated load and unload are safe and idempotent.</p>

  <h2>Capability audit records</h2>
  <p>Direct CLI and service admission append typed rows to <code>audit.jsonl</code> under the configured state directory before provider preparation. A row names the real principal, <code>application_request</code> or <code>child_delegation</code> relation, application and optional parent application κ, requested or delegated capability κ, effective-grant κ, trusted grant source, and <code>allowed</code> or <code>denied</code> outcome. It never copies source documents, storage roots, channels, tokens, headers, or application payload bytes.</p>

  <h2>Build, inspect, and run</h2>
  <p>Layer, capability, and child paths in the source manifest are resolved relative to <code>hologram.json</code>. The source document is retained as metadata, while the canonical application manifest and directory are generated from it.</p>
  <div class="note"><strong>One source schema</strong>Schema v4 is required. It supports prebuilt layer paths, typed Python rootfs and Component source recipes, ordered child application archives, delegated capability documents, and explicit Wasm guest contracts. The build recipe is retained as archive metadata.</div>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "schema_version": 4,
  "primary": 0,
  "requires": "capabilities.json",
  "layers": [
    &#123; "kind": "wasm", "path": "app.wasm", "entry": "holo_run" &#125;,
    &#123; "kind": "view", "path": "ui", "surface": "portable" &#125;
  ]
&#125;

hologram compile hologram.json --output application.holo
hologram --json compile --check hologram.json | jq '&#123;valid, capabilities_kappa&#125;'
hologram run . --input-text 'hello from source' --output-format text
hologram run application.holo --input payload.bin
hologram compile hologram.json --thin --output application.thin.holo
hologram holo import application.holo
hologram --json holo inspect ./application.holo
hologram --json holo plan ./application.holo
hologram holo verify ./application.holo
hologram --json holo inspect blake3:…
hologram --json holo plan blake3:…
hologram holo verify blake3:…
hologram holo load blake3:…
hologram run blake3:… --input payload.bin
hologram holo unload blake3:…</code></pre>
  <p>The repository's <code>examples/wasm-view/</code> project is the executable form of this two-layer manifest. Its frontend uses the same-origin bounded intent endpoint to call its Wasm primary. In Desktop, <strong>Open application</strong> starts an explicit session and presents the View in a separate native window outside the dashboard; repeated messages reuse the prepared primary until the window closes or the user stops it. CLI <code>run</code> remains one-shot and headless, so a View archive reports an unavailable portable surface there. Desktop tests compile the project as a real v4 archive and prove persistent attachment, direct and View invocation, transactional replacement rollback, idempotent detach, and reverse shutdown through a display-independent window-host seam.</p>

  <h3>Compose a child application</h3>
  <p>Compile the child first, then reference its self-contained archive from a schema-v4 parent. The compiler verifies the child archive, extracts its canonical application manifest, compiles the delegated capability source, and embeds the child manifest and verified content blobs in a fat parent. A thin parent retains the identical canonical application identity without embedding blobs.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "schema_version": 4,
  "primary": 0,
  "layers": [
    &#123; "kind": "wasm", "path": "parent.wasm", "entry": "holo_run", "contract": "hologram:guest/core-wasm@1" &#125;
  ],
  "children": [
    &#123;
      "application": "worker.holo",
      "capabilities": "worker-capabilities.json"
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;

hologram compile worker/hologram.json --output parent/worker.holo
hologram --json compile --check parent/hologram.json |
  jq '&#123;valid, child_count&#125;'
hologram --json compile parent/hologram.json --output parent.holo |
  jq '&#123;application_kappa, child_count&#125;'
hologram --json holo inspect parent.holo | jq '.directory.children'</code></pre>
  <div class="note"><strong>Execution boundary</strong>Source composition, physical packaging, recursive κ resolution, canonical child/capability validation, cycle diagnostics, whole-tree limits, capability attenuation, and transactional child execution work now. Startup is depth-first in child-manifest order and stop or rollback is its exact reverse. Only the root primary is automatically invoked; explicitly invoking or independently supervising a child remains a future contract.</div>

  <h3>Desktop watch-and-compile loop</h3>
  <p>Hologram Desktop's <strong>Applications</strong> view can add a local directory containing <code>hologram.json</code>. The trusted Tauri adapter authorizes the selected path and runs fixed compiler commands into a desktop cache outside the source tree. A Tauri-independent workspace crate performs recursive watching, debounce, persistence, and build-state transitions. Each successful archive goes through the normal catalog import path.</p>
  <p>The list and inspector then call <code>holo list</code> and <code>holo inspect</code>. They therefore display the verified archive object κ, canonical application κ, capability κ, directory, logical layers, and physical sections produced by the archive reader—not metadata independently inferred by browser code. A ready watch has a <strong>Run</strong> action; catalog rows expose the same text-input/output panel. <strong>Add .holo</strong> imports an existing archive through the native picker. Execution verifies and downloads the immutable catalog archive to a κ-derived desktop cache path before invoking the direct executor, which supports Wasm and experimental Python rootfs applications while still surfacing provider or authorization failures explicitly.</p>
  <p>When a changed build succeeds, the watch's previous catalog variant is removed; if compilation fails, that last good archive remains inspectable and the project shows the compiler diagnostic. Removing the watch stops future builds and leaves its final immutable archive in the catalog.</p>
  <div class="note"><strong>Trust boundary</strong>The webview receives no general shell command and the background service receives no ambient access to arbitrary host directories. A user selects each source directory through the native picker; the reusable watch engine lives outside <code>src-tauri</code>, while the local adapter owns path authorization and fixed sidecar invocations.</div>

  <h2>Design provenance</h2>
  <p>The one-format principle, ordered layers, capability-attenuated child applications, view surfaces, and fat/thin identity model come from the original <a href="https://github.com/Hologram-Technologies/hologram/blob/94ecb886811115491a77c8229e494965bea03fc2/specs/refactor/03-holo-format.md?plain=1#L19">Hologram v3 application-container design</a>. Hologram Live's verified directory is an additive use of v3's extension seam. <a href="https://github.com/Hologram-Technologies/hologram-live/blob/main/specs/adrs/010-holo-application-plan-and-provider-lifecycle.md">ADR 010</a> fixes the identity vocabulary used by the runtime and APIs.</p>
